  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 322 AM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nEarly morning RAP analysis features an upper-level low pressure \nspinning near Hudson Bay and extending troughing into the north-\ncentral CONUS. Meanwhile, midlevel riding is present over much of \nthe western CONUS, peaking over the Four Corners. At the surface, \nhigh pressure is centered over the Central Plains, extending surface \nridging into our neck of the woods. Closer to home, the cold front \nthat brought spotty convection to the area yesterday is draped just \nsoutheast of the UP. Plenty of lower to midlevel cloud cover is \npresent over the eastern and central UP, but more breaks in the \nclouds are apparent over the far west. Clouds should continue to \nslowly erode from west to east today as the front slowly moves \neastward. Expect temperatures, bottoming out mainly in the 50s early \nthis morning, to peak in the 70s this afternoon. \n\nSurface high pressure slides over the Lower Midwest Thursday, while \naloft, additional midlevel shortwaves rotate through. That said, \nmodel soundings remain too dry for anything more than some stray \ncirrus grazing the area. Increasing southwest flow on the backside \nof the surface ridge and ahead of another approaching Canadian \nshortwave will bring in a surge of warmer air, allowing temperatures \nto climb from morning lows in the 50s to highs in the 80s for most - \neven upper 80s in our typically warmer southerly downsloping spots. \n\nThe aforementioned shortwave reaches the area Friday, with an \nassociated cold front sweeping through during the afternoon and \nevening amid a more moist airmass and an anomalously strong LLJ \nmoving overheard. Along with this, the NBM shows up to 50% chance of \nSBCAPE greater than 500 J/kg along the WI border Friday afternoon, \nand 30 knots of 0-500mb shear. NBM probability of 6-hr precip \nexceeding 0.1\" is around 50% for at least some point on Friday \nafternoon and evening, and the 90th percentile of 6-hr distributions \ntop out at 0.6\" at this point in time. All told, severe weather is \nnot expected for this event at this time.\n\nFurther out, differences in LREF clustering in 500 mb heights begin \naround Day 6 (Aug 8-9) in the timing and strength of trough entering \ninto PNW from Pacific (though strength of this trough is so weak \nsuch that the question is not 'how strong?' but rather 'trough or \nzonal?'). That said, the influence on this region for days 6-9 are \nall very similar, with the Canadian low and Four Corners high still \nin place leading to local NW'ly mid-level flow.",
